<p>The foundations already go further than any school or university benchmark asks (logic, the axioms of sets, choice, the numbers built by cuts). Algebra and the opening of calculus are nearly all earned. Everything from rung 14 on is planned only, which is expected: building is paused at 13.</p>
<h2>The gaps, and where each would go</h2>
<p>Each is a planned addition, not yet a row. Numbers are the ladder's displayed rung numbers.</p>
<p><strong>Five-year math (Common Core, AP, SAT/ACT)</strong>
- Describing data: now planned as the statistics annexes (author, 2026-10-25): 4·stat (mode, range, two-way tables, chance by counting), 5·stat (mean, median, quartiles, box plots, expected value, conditional chance, Bayes), 8·stat (variance, standard deviation, what the mean and median each minimise, correlation bounded by Cauchy–Schwarz), 9·stat (dot plots, histograms, the running total curve, shape and spread), 13·stat (least squares by derivatives, residuals). Rung 30 stands on them.
- Study design: sampling and bias, experiments, simulation, reading reports. A technique page in rung 30, before 30e. (Covers the one SAT gap and the one ACT gap.)
- Inference as AP now teaches it: the t distribution, Type I/II errors, power; χ² for two-way tables (the fall 2026 AP Statistics dropped goodness of fit, which 30f had planned on Mendel's counts: keep Mendel, add the two-way tests). Rung 30.
- Decisions by probability (expected value of a strategy). Rung 30.
- Non-right triangle trigonometry: ½ab sin C, the laws of sines and cosines. 21h.
- Circle theorems: inscribed and central angles, incircle, circumcircle, cyclic quadrilaterals. 18f.
- Parametric, polar and vector-valued functions and their calculus. New rows at 18 and 21, and an extension of 27a.
- Slope fields. 24c. Average value of a function. 16g. Arc length. 21o.
- Series tests: integral test, p-series, alternating series and its error bound, absolute against conditional convergence. 22b and 23b.
- Graph transformations need more than 20i alone; solid geometry and two-variable inequalities have no row.</p>
<p><strong>Science annexes, high school and AP</strong>
- Momentum and impulse: no row anywhere. 16·phys.
- Newton's first and third laws, friction, circular motion, relative motion. 21·phys. Rotational inertia and rolling. 27·phys.
- Electrochemistry (Nernst, ΔG = −nFE, electrolysis), beside 25·chem; reaction mechanisms, 27·then.
- Doppler, photoelectric effect, blackbody radiation, Compton scattering. 24·phys and 35·phys.
- Energy flow between feeding levels, diversity indices, the species–area power law, dose–response curves. 15i, 17·then, a 9·bio call back.
- Climate energy balance and the carbon cycle as a model. 24·then and 27·then.
- Sorting algorithms; integer overflow. A comp annex at 22 and a 4·comp call back.</p>
<p><strong>University mathematics (past rung 30)</strong>
- Analysis: inverse and implicit function theorems; the Lebesgue integral and its convergence theorems (rung 30 says "measure" but has no integral); Banach spaces, Baire, Hahn–Banach, compact operators; distributions and Sobolev spaces.
- Linear algebra: the spectral theorem, Jordan form, the singular value decomposition.
- Groups: quotients and the isomorphism theorems, orbit–stabiliser, Sylow. Rings: ideals and quotient rings (rung 44 names rings without them); modules over a PID; representation theory; commutative and homological algebra; Lie theory.
- Topology and geometry: metric spaces (rung 34 has topology without them), the fundamental group, covering spaces, homology, classification of surfaces, manifolds and differential forms, hyperbolic geometry.
- Complex analysis: Liouville, conformal maps, the Riemann mapping theorem.
- Probability and statistics: martingales, stochastic calculus, inference theory (Neyman–Pearson, sufficiency).
- Differential equations: Picard existence and uniqueness, Sturm–Liouville and Green's functions, characteristics.
- Number theory: quadratic reciprocity, primitive roots, algebraic and analytic number theory.
- Numerical: interpolation, Runge–Kutta, QR and eigenvalue methods.
- Set theory (author, 2026-10-25): Replacement and the ordinals past ω, planned as 29·cb3.</p>
<p><strong>University sciences (past rung 30)</strong>
- Physics: momentum and collisions, Noether, rigid bodies and rotating frames, Hamilton–Jacobi; Laplace and Poisson, fields in matter, radiation, relativistic electrodynamics; dispersion, polarization; quantum past the box and hydrogen (oscillator, tunnelling, spin, perturbation theory, WKB, scattering); condensed matter, nuclear. The partition function has no planned row: statistical mechanics leans entirely on the far-future list.
- Physical chemistry: chemical potential, phase diagrams, steady-state approximation, rotation and vibration, molecular orbitals and Hückel (42·chem).
- Quantitative biology: selection and drift, Leslie matrices, Hill functions and gene circuits, Turing patterns, Hodgkin–Huxley and integrate-and-fire (26·then), PCA, Hopfield networks, sequence alignment.
- CS theory: algorithms (recurrences, sorting, data structures, dynamic programming, greedy, randomized); complexity past P and NP (space, hierarchy, BPP, interactive and zero-knowledge proofs); information theory (source coding, channel capacity); cryptography (symmetric, hashing, Diffie–Hellman).</p>
<h2>Caveats on the sources</h2>
<p>Several official pages could not be opened from this workspace (the MIT catalog, the Stanford bulletin, some College Board PDFs past their first pages, Oxford physics). Each section file names its sources, marks with † what was filled from an earlier edition or a secondary source, and says where a "planned" match is a guess from a row title with no description yet. Re-check those against the official documents before treating the crosswalk as final.</p>
