<p>The ladder now covers Algebra, and Functions apart from exponentials, logarithms and trig. Nothing in Geometry is built: it all waits on rungs 18–21. Statistics waits on rungs 30–31.</p>
<h3>Missing items, by topic</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Non-right triangle trigonometry</strong> (HSG.SRT.D.9, D.10, D.11 (+): ½ab sin C, laws of sines and cosines): add to 21h ("The Two Triangles"). The law of cosines could come back in 27a through the dot product.</li>
<li><strong>Circle theorems</strong> (HSG.C.A.2 inscribed/central angles, HSG.C.A.3 incircle, circumcircle, cyclic quadrilaterals): add to 18f ("Triangles and Parallels by Hand"). 18d or 18·cb13 can carry tangent ⊥ radius.</li>
<li><strong>Describing one-variable data</strong> (HSS.ID.A.1 dot plots, histograms, box plots; HSS.ID.A.3 shape, centre, spread, outliers): a descriptive-data tech page early in 30, before 30e, which should also cover median and IQR for ID.A.2.</li>
<li><strong>Study design and reading reports</strong> (HSS.IC.B.3 surveys, experiments and observational studies, randomisation; HSS.IC.B.6 evaluating reports): fits 30e/30f; 31b could carry the report-reading next to correlation vs causation.</li>
<li><strong>Decisions by probability</strong> (HSS.MD.B.5.b compare strategies by expected value; HSS.MD.B.6 fair decisions by randomising): fits 30b, after expected value. Random devices could also go in 30a.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Close calls to watch (planned, but the planned row does not name the item)</h3>
<ul>
<li>HSG.CO.D.12–13 (routine constructions): 46b is about impossibility; say there what <em>can</em> be built.</li>
<li>HSG.CO.C.11 (parallelograms), HSG.C.A.1 (all circles similar), HSA.REI.D.12 (half-planes), HSS.ID.A.2 (median/IQR), HSS.IC.B.5 (simulation), HSS.MD.B.5 (decisions): each fits its listed row but would need a line added to that row's brief.</li>
</ul></section><section class="cw" id="ap_sat_act"><h2>AP Precalculus, Calculus, Statistics; SAT and ACT</h2><h3>AP, SAT and ACT crosswalk to The Ladder</h3>
<p>Status key: <strong>earned</strong> = a built page teaches or proves it (checked by grepping the page); <strong>planned</strong> = only a planned row covers it; <strong>missing</strong> = no row covers it. Where a topic is partly built and partly planned, the status is the weaker one, and the note says which part is earned. Topic titles are as the College Board publishes them; line items are paraphrases.</p>
<p>Built rungs are 0–13 (with their side rows); everything from rung 14 on is planned, so most of calculus after derivatives, all of trigonometry, exponentials and logarithms, and all statistics are planned.</p>
<h2>AP Precalculus</h2>
<p>Source: College Board, AP Precalculus Course and Exam Description and Course at a Glance — https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap-precalculus-course-and-exam-description.pdf ; https://apcentral.collegeboard.org/media/pdf/ap-precalculus-course-at-a-glance.pdf . Unit 4 is taught but not assessed on the AP Exam.</p>
