<p>Of the 340 line items in scope (564 less 224 out of scope), 72 are earned (21%), 185 planned (54%) and 83 missing (24%). The earned items concentrate in DC circuits (7·phys), thermochemistry (11·chem), stoichiometry (5·chem), 1-D kinematics (9·phys, 13·phys), rates (13·chem) and logic (1e, 4·comp). Most AP Physics 2 and C: E&amp;M items wait on 34·phys, and most of AP Chemistry units 7–9 wait on 17·chem and 25·chem, whose rows are titles only.</p>
<h3>Main missing items, and the annex each would fit</h3>
<p><strong>Mechanics (biggest gap)</strong>
- <strong>Momentum and impulse</strong> (PS2-2, PS2-3, P1/C-Mech unit 4): no row at all. Add to <strong>16·phys</strong>, where impulse is ∫F dt beside work as ∫F dx, or give it a 16·phys companion page.
- <strong>Newton's first and third laws, friction, free-body work, relative motion, circular motion</strong> (P1/C 1.4, 2.3, 2.4, 2.7, 2.10): fit <strong>21·phys "Forces as arrows"</strong>, with v²/r once 21j's radians exist.
- <strong>Rotational inertia and rolling</strong> (P1/C 5.4, 6.5): fit <strong>27·phys</strong>, after 16's integrals.
- <strong>Energy in SHM, drag and terminal velocity</strong> (7.4, C 2.9): fit <strong>24·phys</strong>. Drag is dv/dt = g − kv, solved by 24a.
- <strong>Density and buoyancy</strong> (P1 8.1, 8.3): fit an <strong>8·phys</strong> or <strong>12·phys</strong> call-back (Archimedes again), or <strong>28·phys</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Waves and modern physics</strong>
- <strong>Doppler effect, polarization, thin-film interference</strong> (P2 14.3, 14.5, 14.9): Doppler fits <strong>24·phys</strong>; thin film and polarization fit <strong>32·phys</strong>.
- <strong>Photoelectric effect, blackbody radiation, Compton scattering</strong> (P2 15.4–15.6): fit <strong>35·phys</strong>, with Compton possibly in <strong>29·phys</strong> (relativistic momentum).
- <strong>Redshift and Hubble's law</strong> (ESS1-2): fit <strong>31·then</strong> (least-squares line).</p>
<p><strong>Electricity and magnetism</strong>
- <strong>Resistivity ρL/A</strong> (P2/C 11.3): a small add to <strong>7·phys</strong>.
- <strong>LR circuits</strong> (C 13.5): fit <strong>24·then</strong>, beside RC.
- <strong>Dielectrics</strong> (C 10.4): fit <strong>34·phys</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Chemistry</strong>
- <strong>Composition: percent by mass, empirical formula, isotope averages, mixtures</strong> (Chem 1.2–1.4): fit a <strong>5·chem call-back</strong> or extension.
- <strong>Ionic formulas by lcm</strong> (Chem 1.8): also a <strong>5·chem call-back</strong>.
- <strong>Bond enthalpies</strong> (PS1-4, Chem 6.7): an <strong>11·chem</strong> extension.
- <strong>Potential-energy curve of a bond</strong> (Chem 2.2): fit <strong>14f</strong>'s annex as a minimum.
- <strong>Reaction mechanisms, pre-equilibrium, multistep profiles</strong> (Chem 5.7–5.10): fit <strong>27·then</strong> (coupled rate equations), or a new <strong>24·chem</strong>.
- <strong>Non-first-order rate laws</strong> (Chem 5.2): a <strong>13·chem</strong> extension.
- <strong>Titration curves and buffer capacity</strong> (Chem 8.5, 8.10): fit <strong>17·chem</strong> or <strong>25·chem</strong>.
- <strong>Electrochemistry: cells, ΔG = −nFE, Nernst, electrolysis by Faraday</strong> (Chem 9.8–9.11): no row covers it. A new <strong>25·chem</strong>-adjacent annex fits (logs and free energy are ready there). Electrolysis could also be a <strong>7·phys × 5·chem</strong> call-back.
- <strong>Coupled reactions and thermodynamic versus kinetic control</strong> (Chem 9.4, 9.7): fit <strong>25·chem</strong>.
- <strong>Deviation from ideal gases</strong> (Chem 3.6): fit <strong>15·chem</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Biology and environmental science</strong>
- <strong>Energy flow, 10% rule, NPP, biomagnification</strong> (LS2-4, Bio 8.2, APES 1.8–1.10, 8.8): fit <strong>15i</strong> geometric sums, or a <strong>17·bio</strong> extension.
- <strong>Diversity indices</strong> (Bio 8.6, APES 2.1): fit <strong>17·then</strong> (logs; Shannon) or <strong>30·then</strong>.
- <strong>Species–area law S = cA^z</strong> (APES 2.3): an easy <strong>9·bio</strong> call-back.
- <strong>Enzyme inhibition and environmental effects</strong> (Bio 3.2): a <strong>10·bio</strong> extension.
- <strong>Linkage and recombination frequency</strong> (Bio 5.4): fit <strong>30a</strong> or <strong>30·bio</strong>.
- <strong>Feedback and homeostasis as stable fixed points</strong> (LS1-3, Bio 4.4): fit <strong>24e</strong>'s annex.
- <strong>Maximum sustainable yield at K/2</strong> (APES 5.8): fit <strong>24d</strong> with <strong>14h</strong>.
- <strong>Dose–response and LD50 on log dose</strong> (APES 8.12–8.13): fit <strong>17·then</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Earth science</strong>
- <strong>Energy-balance climate model and greenhouse effect</strong> (ESS2-4, APES 9.3): fit <strong>24·then</strong>.
- <strong>Quantitative carbon-cycle box model</strong> (ESS2-6, APES 1.4): fit <strong>27·then</strong>.
- <strong>Insolation by cos θ</strong> (APES 4.7): fit <strong>21·then "Angles enter"</strong>.</p>
<p><strong>Computing</strong>
- <strong>Sorting, merge sort, fixed-width integers and overflow, parallel speedup</strong> (CSA 1.5, 4.15, 4.17; CSP 4.3): none is planned. The natural home is a new <strong>22·comp</strong> sibling ("sorting and how fast", alongside 22d's big O), with overflow as a <strong>4·comp call-back</strong>.</p></section><section class="cw" id="university"><h2>University mathematics</h2><h3>Crosswalk: undergraduate and graduate mathematics (MIT, Stanford, Cambridge, Oxford)</h3>
<p>Benchmark for the upper end of the ladder. Each row is one topic, merged across institutions. Wording is paraphrased from the official schedules, not quoted.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul>
<li>MIT, Course 18 pure option requirements (18.03, 18.100, 18.701, 18.702, 18.901, one of 18.101/102/103, one seminar such as 18.904 or 18.784): https://math.mit.edu/academics/undergrad/major/course18/pure.php</li>
<li>MIT, degree requirements for all three options, general, applied and pure (18.04/18.112, 18.06/18.700, 18.200, 18.300, applied groups including 18.600, 18.650, 18.404, 18.330, 18.303): https://math.mit.edu/academics/undergrad/major/old-degree-requirements.html</li>
<li>MIT, qualifying exams: an oral exam on three topics (one major, two minor), each about one graduate semester's worth, set with the examiners. No fixed syllabus is published: https://math.mit.edu/academics/grad/timeline/quals.php</li>
<li>MIT catalog degree chart (https://catalog.mit.edu/degree-charts/mathematics-course-18/) could not be fetched in this session. Subject contents for 18.01–18.03, 18.06, 18.112, 18.600/18.650, 18.781, 18.905 and 18.950 come from their standard catalog descriptions and were not checked against the page.</li>
<li>Stanford, math major requirements (now stated as counts: eight 100-level courses including Math 56, calculus series 51/52/53 or 61CM/62CM/63CM, electives): https://mathematics.stanford.edu/academics/undergraduate-students/math-major</li>
<li>Stanford, PhD qualifying exams. Three areas are offered: algebra, real analysis, and geometry and topology, and a student passes two. Complex analysis is not currently a separate qual. The per-area syllabi sit on Google Drive and could not be opened, so qual contents here are inferred from the area names and the matching courses: https://mathematics.stanford.edu/academics/graduate-students/phd-program/phd-qualifying-exams</li>
<li>Stanford course contents (51/52/53, 61CM–63CM, 104, 113, 115, 171, 120, 121, 143, 145, 147, 148, 151, 152, 162) come from standard bulletin titles and descriptions. The bulletin (https://bulletin.stanford.edu) could not be fetched.</li>
<li>Cambridge, Mathematical Tripos schedules 2024–25 (Parts IA, IB, II): https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/undergrad/course/schedules.pdf</li>
<li>Cambridge, Part III guide to courses, taken here as graduate level (areas: algebra, algebraic geometry, analysis and PDEs, combinatorics, differential geometry and topology, foundations, number theory, probability, statistics, applied): https://www.maths.cam.ac.uk/part-iii-guide-courses</li>
<li>Oxford, Prelims synopses 2025–26: https://courses.maths.ox.ac.uk/mod/resource/view.php?id=61298 (folder: https://courses.maths.ox.ac.uk/mod/folder/view.php?id=55865)</li>
<li>Oxford, Part A synopses 2023–24: https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/Part%20A%20Mathematics%20Synopses%202023-24.pdf</li>
<li>Oxford, Part B synopses 2024–25: https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/inline-files/Part%20B%20Mathematics%20Synopses%202024-25_0.pdf</li>
<li>Oxford, Part C unit list (2016–17, the newest full list that could be opened; the current lists are in the folder above), taken here as graduate level: https://www.maths.ox.ac.uk/system/files/attachments/PartC2016-17_final_0.pdf</li>
</ul>
<h2>How to read the tables</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Who requires it</strong>: MIT, Stan (Stanford), Cam (Cambridge), Ox (Oxford). Cambridge Part IA and IB and Oxford Prelims and Part A core count as required. Part II, Part B and Part A options are listed as offered.</li>
<li><strong>Level</strong>: <em>UG core</em> means the first two years or a required subject in the major. <em>UG advanced</em> means a third-year option or elective. <em>grad qual</em> means the subject is on a PhD qualifying exam, or is MIT first-year graduate material, Cambridge Part III or Oxford Part C.</li>
<li><strong>Ladder page(s)</strong>: labels from LADDER_ROWS.tsv. "BEYOND" is the single far-future entry in roadmap.py (after rung 46: physics, discrete mathematics and logic, with general relativity, statistical mechanics and quantum computing named).</li>
<li><strong>Status</strong>: <em>earned</em> means a built page covers the topic, checked by grepping the built HTML. <em>planned</em> means a planned row covers it; "(partial)" marks a row that covers only part of the topic, or names the topic only in its rung title. <em>beyond</em> means it appears only in BEYOND. <em>missing</em> means it appears nowhere.</li>
</ul>
