<h2>Summary</h2>
<h3>Counts by status</h3>
<p>There are 200 rows in all (recounted 2026-10-26 after the plan for rungs 22–42 placed every missing row; "beyond" now means the graduate region past rung 47). Rows marked "(partial)" are counted under the status they carry.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Status</th>
<th>Rows</th>
<th>of which partial</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>earned</td>
<td>24</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>planned</td>
<td>165</td>
<td>16</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>beyond</td>
<td>11</td>
<td>–</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>missing</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>–</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<h3>Counts by level and status</h3>
<p>A row with two levels, such as "UG core / grad qual", is counted at its higher level.</p>
<div class="tw"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Level</th>
<th>Rows</th>
<th>earned</th>
<th>planned</th>
<th>beyond</th>
<th>missing</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>UG core</td>
<td>107</td>
<td>18</td>
<td>89</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>0</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>UG advanced</td>
<td>50</td>
<td>4</td>
<td>46</td>
<td>0</td>
<td>0</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>grad qual</td>
<td>43</td>
<td>2</td>
<td>30</td>
<td>11</td>
<td>0</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>What this shows:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Ladder's foundations already go past what any of the four universities requires: logic, axiomatic set theory, choice, and building the number systems.</li>
<li>First-year analysis and calculus are earned up to the derivative and planned through rung 23.</li>
<li>The third-year and qualifying-exam core is mostly missing: measure-theoretic analysis, functional analysis, the algebra of quotients, modules and representations, and algebraic topology.</li>
<li>Many planned rows cover a topic only partly. They name the idea, such as "measure" or "rings and fields", without the theorems the syllabi examine.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Main missing topics, with where they would fit past rung 30</h3>
<p>Superseded on 2026-10-26: the plan for rungs 22–42 (design/roadmap.py) gives every topic below a planned row, and the tables above name it. The list is kept as the reasoning that led there.</p>
<p><strong>Analysis</strong>
- Inverse and implicit function theorems: a sub-row of 28, or a rung just after it.
- Lebesgue integral, convergence theorems, Fubini–Tonelli, Lp spaces, Radon–Nikodym: rung 30's measure half, written out as its own rung before probability (30a onward) leans on it.
- Banach spaces, Baire category and its three consequences, Hahn–Banach, compact operators and their spectral theorem: a functional-analysis rung after 31, as its sequel.
- Distributions and Sobolev spaces: a rung after 33, so PDEs get weak solutions.
- Weierstrass approximation: a sub-row of 31 or 32.</p>
<p><strong>Algebra</strong>
- Spectral theorem, Jordan form, Cayley–Hamilton, dual spaces and bilinear forms, SVD: a second linear-algebra rung between 27 and 31, which 31 and 32 then use.
- Homomorphisms, quotient groups, isomorphism theorems, group actions with orbit–stabiliser, Sylow, free groups: sub-rows added to 19, plus a "groups II" rung before 41.
- Modules over a PID and their structure theorem: a sub-row of 40, which then also gives Jordan form.
- Representation theory of finite groups: a rung after 41.
- Commutative algebra and homological algebra: rungs after 42, using category theory.
- Lie algebras: after Lie groups (see below).</p>
<p><strong>Geometry and topology</strong>
- Classification of surfaces and Euler characteristic, fundamental group and covering spaces, homology and cohomology: an algebraic-topology rung or two right after 34, built on groups (19) and quotients.
- Hyperbolic geometry: a sub-row of 29.
- Smooth manifolds, differential forms, the general Stokes theorem: a manifolds rung after 34, uniting 28d and 29.
- Riemannian curvature: the same manifolds rung, which also prepares the general relativity in BEYOND.
- Lie groups: after manifolds and 27.
- Algebraic curves and varieties: after 40 and 42.</p>
<p><strong>Complex analysis</strong>
- Conformal maps and Möbius transformations, the Riemann mapping theorem, harmonic functions, analytic continuation and Riemann surfaces: a second complex rung after 26, perhaps after 34 so compactness arguments (normal families) are available.</p>
<p><strong>Probability and statistics</strong>
- Generating functions and branching processes: sub-rows of 30.
- Continuous-time chains and Poisson processes: after 30g.
- Measure-theoretic probability and martingales, then Brownian motion and stochastic calculus: a rung following the Lebesgue rung.
- Neyman–Pearson, sufficiency, maximum likelihood theory, Bayesian inference, GLMs: a statistics rung after 31.
- Mathematical finance: after stochastic calculus.</p>
<p><strong>Differential equations</strong>
- Picard existence and uniqueness: a sub-row of 24, or a later rung once contraction mapping (metric spaces, 34) is in hand.
- Sturm–Liouville and Green's functions, Frobenius series, Laplace's equation and the maximum principle, characteristics: sub-rows of 33, or a second PDE rung.
- Functional-analytic PDE: after the functional-analysis and distributions rungs.</p>
<p><strong>Number theory</strong>
- Primitive roots, quadratic reciprocity, continued fractions and Pell's equation, primality tests: sub-rows of 39, or a "number theory II" rung after it.
- Algebraic number theory: after 40 and 41.
- Analytic number theory (prime number theorem, zeta): after the second complex rung.
- Elliptic curves and modular forms: graduate, after algebraic geometry.</p>
<p><strong>Logic and foundations</strong>
- Forcing and large cardinals: after 35 and 36, a natural fit for BEYOND's "logic" rungs.
- Lambda calculus: a sub-row of 37.</p>
<p><strong>Applied and numerical</strong>
- Interpolation and orthogonal polynomials, Runge–Kutta and stiffness, QR and eigenvalue algorithms: a numerical-analysis rung after 31 and 32.
- Game theory: a sub-row of 38.</p>
<p><strong>Discrete mathematics</strong>
- Pigeonhole and inclusion–exclusion: sub-rows of an early counting page (4k), or of 38.
- Generating functions: after 23, as power series used for counting.
- Ramsey and probabilistic combinatorics: after 38 and 30.</p></section><section class="cw" id="university_science"><h2>University sciences</h2><h3>Crosswalk: university-level math-heavy science vs. The Ladder</h3>
<p>Scope: the undergraduate core (first to third year, some upper-level electives) in physics, physical chemistry, quantitative biology and theoretical computer science at MIT, Stanford, Cambridge and Oxford. High school and AP science are covered in a separate section. Everything below paraphrases catalog descriptions; nothing is quoted.</p>
<p>Ladder labels come from <code>LADDER_ROWS.tsv</code> (the canonical numbering). Note that <code>roadmap.py</code> numbers its planned rungs one lower than the TSV for rungs past 20 (for example its "21·comp" entropy page is the TSV's 22·comp; its 39 is the TSV's 40). This file uses the TSV labels throughout.</p>
<h2>Status key</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>earned</strong>: a built page covers the topic; confirmed by searching the built HTML.</li>
<li><strong>planned</strong>: a planned row (math rung, subrow or annex) names the topic or plainly contains it.</li>
<li><strong>beyond</strong>: only the BEYOND list covers it ("after 41: general relativity, statistical mechanics, quantum computing among them").</li>
<li><strong>missing</strong>: no row or BEYOND entry covers it.</li>
<li><strong>out of scope</strong>: laboratory or non-mathematical content.</li>
</ul>
<p>"Who" tags: MIT, Stan (Stanford), Cam (Cambridge), Ox (Oxford). A tag marked † comes from a course title or general knowledge of the course, not from a description fetched this session.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<p>Physics
- MIT subject listing, Course 8 (8.01–8.09, 8.044, 8.08, 8.20, 8.033, 8.286, 8.962, 8.592): https://catalog.mit.edu/subjects/8/8.pdf
- Stanford Physics bulletin (archived 2015 ExploreDegrees PDF; PHYSICS 41–46, 61–67, 110, 120–121, 130–134, 170–171, 262): https://swap.stanford.edu/was/20151211033644mp_/http:/exploredegrees.stanford.edu/schoolofhumanitiesandsciences/physics/physics.pdf
- Stanford Physics introductory sequences (says majors now take 61/71/81): https://physics.stanford.edu/undergraduate/undergraduate-courses
- Cambridge Physics undergraduate course list (Part IA, IB, II core): https://www.phy.cam.ac.uk/study/undergraduate/undergraduate-courses/
- Cambridge NST Part IA Physics: https://www.natsci.tripos.cam.ac.uk/node/156
- Cambridge NST Part IB Physics A: https://www.natsci.tripos.cam.ac.uk/node/176
- Cambridge NST Part IB Physics B: https://www.natsci.tripos.cam.ac.uk/node/161
- Oxford Physics core structure (CP1–CP4, A1–A3, B1–B7), via a summary of the official course handbooks, since the official Oxford pages could not be fetched: https://cirosantilli.com/physics-course-of-the-university-of-oxford</p>
<p>Physical chemistry
- MIT 5.60 Thermodynamics and Kinetics, OCW lecture list: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-60-thermodynamics-kinetics-spring-2008/pages/lecture-notes/
- MIT 5.61 Physical Chemistry (quantum), OCW syllabus: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-61-physical-chemistry-fall-2017/pages/syllabus
- Stanford CHEM 171, Foundations of Physical Chemistry: https://bulletin.stanford.edu/courses/1122861
- (Cambridge and Oxford chemistry syllabi could not be retrieved; their tags are left off.)</p>
<p>Quantitative biology
- MIT 7.32 Systems Biology: https://stuff.mit.edu/afs/sipb/project/course-search/web_scripts/search/urls-fa18/cat/7.32.html
- MIT 8.592 Statistical Physics in Biology, outline: https://web.mit.edu/8.592/www/admin/outline.html
- MIT 9.40 Introduction to Neural Computation, OCW lecture list: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/9-40-introduction-to-neural-computation-spring-2018/pages/lecture-notes/
- Stanford BIO 287 Mathematical Population Biology: https://bulletin.stanford.edu/courses/2226473
- Stanford Human Population Biology (J. H. Jones; demography, epidemics): https://web.stanford.edu/~jhj1/teaching/human_population.html
- Cambridge NST Part IA Mathematical Biology (no topic list published on the page): https://www.natsci.tripos.cam.ac.uk/node/96</p>
<p>Computer science theory
- MIT 6.006 / 6.1210 Introduction to Algorithms, OCW: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/electrical-engineering-and-computer-science/6-006-introduction-to-algorithms-spring-2020
- MIT 18.404J Theory of Computation, OCW syllabus: https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-404j-theory-of-computation-fall-2020/pages/syllabus
- MIT Math theoretical CS subjects (18.400, 18.404, 18.410, 18.424 information theory): https://math.mit.edu/academics/undergrad/subjects/184x.html
- Stanford CS 154 Introduction to the Theory of Computation: https://bulletin.stanford.edu/courses/1056821
- Stanford CS 161 Design and Analysis of Algorithms: https://22-23.bulletin.stanford.edu/courses/1056871
- Stanford CS 255 Introduction to Cryptography: https://bulletin.stanford.edu/courses/1057791 and https://crypto.stanford.edu/~dabo/courses/cs255_winter21/overview.html
- Stanford EE 276 Information Theory: https://web.stanford.edu/class/ee276/index.html
- Cambridge CST Part IB Complexity Theory: https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/2526/Complexity
- Cambridge CST course pages (Part II Information Theory, Cryptography, Quantum Computing, Probability and Computation): https://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/teaching/1920/cst/</p>
<p>Ladder
- <code>build/curriculum/LADDER_ROWS.tsv</code>, <code>build/roadmap.py</code> (BEYOND), built HTML in <code>artifact-files/b182fb5e-…/</code>.</p>
