<td><strong>4</strong></td>
<td><strong>173</strong></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>What is earned is almost all foundations (logic, sets, induction, counting) or the introductory end of a university course: kinematics, DC circuits, calorimetry, first-order rates, Michaelis–Menten. That fits a ladder whose built pages stop at rung 13. Since the plan of 2026-10-26, every row once missing has a planned home in rungs 22–42 or sits in the graduate region past 42.</p>
<h2>Main missing topics, with a suggested home</h2>
<p>Superseded on 2026-10-26: the plan for rungs 22–42 (design/roadmap.py) gives every topic below a planned row, and the tables above name it. The list is kept as the reasoning that led there.</p>
<p>Physics
- Momentum and collisions: a 16·phys companion, since impulse is the integral of force.
- Noether's theorem and canonical transformations: inside 29·then, right after least action.
- Rigid bodies (inertia tensor) and rotating frames: a 27·phys sequel once 27d's eigenvectors exist.
- Laplace and Poisson equations: add to rung 33 (33·then), alongside heat and waves.
- Fields in matter, multipoles, radiation, relativistic electrodynamics: a second E&amp;M annex after 34·phys, at rung 35 or 36.
- Dispersion and group velocity; polarization: 32·phys, which already handles spectra and interference.
- Quantum oscillator, tunnelling, uncertainty and wave packets: 35·phys (the oscillator could open at 32 with Fourier).
- Spin, angular momentum, identical particles, perturbation theory, WKB, scattering: a second quantum annex, "35·phys II", or a BEYOND entry for quantum mechanics II.
- Langevin and Fokker–Planck: 33·then, combining 30d's random walks with 33a's diffusion equation.
- Condensed matter (lattices, bands) and nuclear physics: name them in BEYOND, as the core courses all expect them.</p>
<p>Physical chemistry
- Chemical potential, phase diagrams and Clausius–Clapeyron: 28·then (one-variable slopes of a phase boundary come from 28a).
- Steady-state and pre-equilibrium approximations: 24·then with Arrhenius, as a pair of ODEs.
- Rigid rotor and harmonic oscillator as molecular rotation and vibration: 35·phys, feeding 32·then's spectroscopy.
- Molecular orbitals and Hückel theory: 42·chem, since Hückel energies are eigenvalues of a molecule's graph.
- Photochemistry: a short 35·phys or 32·then note.</p>
<p>Quantitative biology
- Selection and drift (Wright–Fisher, diffusion limit): extend 30·bio using 30g's Markov chains.
- Leslie matrices: a 27·bio annex on eigenvectors (27d).
- Hill functions and gene switches/oscillators: 24·then or 27·then (fixed points from 24e, systems from 27).
- Turing patterns: 33·bio, next to Fick's second law.
- Integrate-and-fire and Hodgkin–Huxley: 26·then, which already treats the membrane as a circuit.
- PCA, perceptrons and Hopfield networks: a 31·bio or 31·comp annex (projections at 31a, gradient descent at 28f).
- Sequence alignment: a 42·comp annex, together with dynamic programming.</p>
<p>Computer science theory
- Recurrences, divide and conquer, sorting and its lower bound, dynamic programming, greedy, amortized analysis, data structures: a run of comp annexes at 22·comp (recurrences alongside big O at 22d) and 42·comp (algorithms on graphs and tables).
- Randomized algorithms and BPP: a 30·comp annex.
- Space complexity, hierarchy theorems, recursion theorem: 41c or a further 37 subrow.
- Interactive and zero-knowledge proofs: 43·comp, alongside signatures.
- Source coding and channel capacity: a 30·comp or 31·comp annex continuing 22·comp's entropy.
- Symmetric cryptography, hashing, Diffie–Hellman: 43·comp, before RSA's digital signatures.</p></section>
